  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 130 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nSaturday through Thursday:\n\nSynopsis: An energetic large scale pattern will continue into the \nlong term period. A surface cold front will vacate to the south and \neast of the region Saturday afternoon or evening, bringing chances \nfor a few isolated showers and thunderstorms in the event sufficient \nsunshine occurs earlier in the day. An intermediate area of high \npressure will move through the western Great Lakes Saturday night \nthrough Sunday, bringing pleasant conditions for the end of the \nweekend. A second upper disturbance and cold front will move in from \nthe Northern Plains Sunday night through Monday night, bringing \nperiods of showers of thunderstorms. Will be monitoring trends for \nsevere weather and locally heavy rain potential, particularly in any \nthunderstorms occurring Monday evening/night. The responsible cold \nfront will clear south of the area during the day Tuesday, with \narriving high pressure bringing pleasant conditions through \nWednesday night. Rain chances may return by Thursday, though global \nguidance remains split over whether this potential will materialize. \n   \nSaturday: Will be monitoring timing trends regarding cold frontal \nprogression through southern Wisconsin, as a slower/later evolution \nwould open a window for isolated storm development during the \nafternoon/early evening. Storm formation would also be dependent on \na period of sufficient sunshine & surface heating in the wake of \nearlier day showers/storms, which adds further uncertainty to the \ndevelopment picture. Range of timing & sky condition solutions \nremain apparent in available forecast guidance, which is driving low \n(~10-15%) overall precip probabilities in the NBM. Given the noted \nuncertainties, have trended precip probabilities toward that of the \nNBM in the afternoon update. Will continue to refine this aspect of \nthe forecast through Saturday morning.\n\nSunday night through Monday morning: Broad warm advection will be \nincreasing regionally ahead of the next disturbance, with a \nseasonably strong low level jet helping to focus nocturnal shower \nand storm development across the mid-upper Mississippi Valley. \nAnticipating activity making it into southern Wisconsin near/after \nmidnight & lingering through at least daybreak Monday. With larger \nscale thermodynamic recovery ongoing through this time frame, \nexpecting instability values to be sufficient for thunder but a \ntouch marginal for severe weather hazards. As such, expecting any \nthunderstorm hazards to be related to locally heavy downpours given \nincreasing precipitable water values in the 1.2-1.5\u201d range. Will be \nmonitoring for any training signals in model guidance as this \nportion of the period draws closer. \n\nMonday evening through Monday night: A second window of shower and \nstorm potential will open as a surface warm sector (if it hasn\u2019t \nalready) spreads into southern Wisconsin in advance of a cold front \n& approaching upper wave. Available deterministic precip forecasts & \nthe broader pattern point toward storm initiation occurring during \nthe afternoon over Iowa, with activity then moving toward southern \nWisconsin after sunset. Mesoscale details will become clearer \nthrough this weekend, though large front-parallel shear orientations \nimply likely upscale growth in storms as they transition into the \nCWA. Despite the parallel nature of front-shear vector alignment, \nsufficient deep layer shear magnitudes & instability, in addition to \na steadily increasing low level jet, would be factors supporting \nstorm maintenance into the overnight hours. Will thus be evaluating \narriving high-res guidance for possible severe weather hazards into \nthis weekend. Even higher precipitable water values in the 1.5-\n1.75\u201d+ range would support additional potential for locally heavy \nrainfall and some hydro impacts as well, particularly in locations \nexperiencing training.\n\nQuigley",
